The flag is the value in the `NickName` column of that row.

```php
$stmt = $conn->prepare("SELECT id, name, eid, salary, birth, ssn, address, email, nickname, Password FROM credential WHERE name = ? and Password = ?");
$stmt->bind_param("ss", $input_uname, $hashed_pwd);
$stmt->execute();
$result = $stmt->get_result();
```
With the data bound as parameters instead of concatenated into the SQL text, an injection payload is treated as literal data and the query no longer matches any row. `getinfo.php` then shows "No matching account found." - the else branch added for this exercise - instead of printing blank fields.
